                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            (so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is the strict inverse of
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            If you think of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            as the category of (strict) categories and functors, then this is the usual notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            in a category. This is the most obvious notion of equivalence of categories and the first to be considered, but it is simply too strong for the purposes to which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            is put. For example, here are some basic and important equivalences of categories that are not isomorphisms:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Let
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                be the category of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+sets"} pointed sets
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                be the category of sets and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+functions"} partial functions
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo :
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F:C\to D
                takes a pointed set to its subset of non-basepoint elements, and a pointed function to the induced partial function on these (which is defined on those elements not sent to the basepoint). See the section “The category of sets and partial functions” in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+function"} partial function
                for this equivalence.
            %li
              %p
                Let
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                be the category of finite-dimensional
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
                over a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                be the category whose objects are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
