                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            (so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is a strong equivalence from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            to
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is called a
            %strong weak inverse
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            (so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is a weak inverse of
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            Note that an isomorphism is precisely a strong equivalence in which the natural isomorphisms are
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+natural+transformation"} identity natural transformation
          %p
            If you think of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-category"} strict 2-category
            of (strict) categories, functors, and
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
            in a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} category
          .query
            %p If possible, use or modify the counterexample to isomorphism to show how choice follows if strong equivalence is assumed correct.
          %h3#WeakEquivalence Weak equivalence
          %p
            Two categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            are
            %strong weakly equivalent
            if there exist a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\colon X \to D
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\colon X \to C
            that are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+surjective+functor"} essentially surjective
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functor"} fully faithful
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is a
            %strong weak equivalence
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            (so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is a weak equivalence from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"} span
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,F,G)
            a
            %strong weak equivalence
            between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %p
            A strict functor with a weak inverse is necessarily essentially surjective and fully faithful; the converse is equivalent to the axiom of choice. Thus any strong equivalence becomes a weak equivalence in which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is taken to be either
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            (or even built symmetrically out of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            if you're so inclined); a weak equivalence becomes a strong equivalence using the axiom of choice to find weak inverses and composing across
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            If you think of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            as the model category of categories and functors with the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+model+structure"} canonical model structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalence"} weak equivalence
            in a model category.
          %h3#Anaequivalence Anaequivalence
          %p
            Two categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            are
            %strong anaequivalent
            if there exist
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anafunctors"} anafunctors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\colon C \to D
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\colon D \to C
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F G
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G F
            are each ananaturally isomorphic (isomorphic in the relevant
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anafunctor+category"} anafunctor category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+anafunctor"} identity anafunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is an
            %strong anaequivalence
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            (so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is an anaequivalence from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            to
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is called an
            %strong anainverse
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            (so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is an anainverse of
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalence+of+internal+categories"} weak equivalence of internal categories
          %p
            Any strict functor is an anafunctor, so any strong equivalence is an anaequivalence. Using the axiom of choice, any anafunctor is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ananatural+isomorphism"} ananaturally isomorphic
            to a strict functor, so any anaequivalence defines a strong equivalence. Using the definition of an anafunctor as an appropriate span of strict functors, a weak equivalence defines two anafunctors which form an anaequivalence; conversely, either anafunctor in an anaequivalence is (as a span) a weak equivalence.
          %p
            If you think of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            of categories, anafunctors, and
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ananatural+transformation"} ananatural transformation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
            in a
            = succeed "-category." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %p
            We can also regard the
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            above as obtained from the
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Str
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Str Cat
            of strict categories, strict functors, and natural transformations by formally inverting the weak equivalences as in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
          %h3#fully_faithful_essentially_surjective_functors Fully faithful essentially surjective functors
          %p
            Finally, there are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful"} fully faithful
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+surjective+functors"} essentially surjective functors
          #ViaEssentiallySurjectiveAndFullyFaithful.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p Assume the ambient context is one of the following:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
                  with the
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
                  or
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category"} internal
                  category theory with “functor” meaning
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anafunctor"} anafunctor
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher-level+foundations"} higher-level foundations
                  with “category” meaning
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+category"} univalent category
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \colon \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{D}
              be a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                  is part of an equivalence of categories in the sense of def.
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EquivalenceViaInverseFunctor"}
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                  is
                %ol
                  %li
                    %p
                      an
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+surjective+functor"} essentially surjective functor
                      and
                  %li
                    %p
                      a
                      = succeed "." do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+functor"} fully faithful functor
          %h3#remarks Remarks
          %p
            Note that
            %em weak
            inverses go with
            %em strong
            equivalences. The terminology isn't entirely inconsistent (weak inverses contrast with
            %em strict
            ones, while weak equivalences contrast with
            %em strong
            ones) but developed at different times. The prefix ‘ana‑’ developed last and is perfectly consistent.
          %p
            If you accept the axiom of choice, then you don't have to worry about the different kinds of equivalence (as long as you don't use isomorphism). This is not just a question of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal contexts
          %p
            It's also possible to use foundations (such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FOLDS"} FOLDS
            = succeed "-Lab," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %h2#adjoint_equivalence Adjoint equivalence
          %p
            Any equivalence can be improved to an
            = succeed ":" do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+equivalence"} adjoint equivalence
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} triangle identities
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is called
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+the"} the
            %strong adjoint inverse
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            (so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is the adjoint inverse of
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
          %ul
            %li
              a functor with the
              %em property
              of being a
              %em general
              equivalence or
            %li
              a functor with the
              %em structure
              of being an
              %em adjoint
              equivalence (that is, a functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              and a pair of natural isomorphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mi G
                    %mo ≅
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F G \cong 1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi G
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \cong G F
              satisfying the triangle identities),
          %p whereas considering
          %ul
            %li
              a functor with the
              %em structure
              of being a
              %em general
              equivalence (that is, merely a functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              and a pair of natural isomorphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mi G
                    %mo ≅
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F G \cong 1
              and
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi G
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \cong G F
          %p
            is fraught with peril. For instance, an adjoint inverse is unique up to unique isomorphism (much as a strict inverse is unique up to equality), while a weak inverse or anainverse is not. Including adjoint equivalences is also the only way to make a higher-categorical structure completely
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+definition+of+higher+category"} algebraic
          %h3#an_example_of_a_nonadjoint_equivalence An example of a non-adjoint equivalence
          %p
            Identify a group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            with its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Any equivalence
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo :
                      %mi H
                      %mo ⇆
                      %mi H
                      %mo :
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : H \leftrightarrows H : G
                of a group with itself comprises two automorphisms
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo ,
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F, G
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F G
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G F
                are inner. The unit and counit are the group elements
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mi ρ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_{\rho}
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi GF
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mi ρ
                      %mi k
                      %msubsup
                        %mi g
                        %mi ρ
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GF(k) = g_{\rho} k g_{\rho}^{-1}
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mi σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_{\sigma}
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi FG
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msubsup
                        %mi g
                        %mi σ
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mi k
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mi σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FG(k) = g_{\sigma}^{-1} k g_{\sigma}
                for any
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi H
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in H
            %li
              %p
                Any equivalence of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
                with itself where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                are themselves also inner is an adjoint equivalence.
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
                has trivial center, then any equivalence of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
                with itself is an adjoint equivalence.
            %li
              %p
                To obtain a non-adjoint equivalence, we therefore need a group
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
                with nontrivial center and nontrivial outer automorphisms, such that we can pick two whose products are inner.
            %li
              %p
                So take
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                      %mo =
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H = K
                the Klein 4-group. This is a product of abelian groups, so abelian, so is its own center. In fact, it’s
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mn 2
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mo ×
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mn 2
                        %mi ℤ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}/2 \mathbb{Z} \times \mathbb{Z} / 2 \mathbb{Z}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo =
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F = G
                the automorphism which interchanges coordinates.
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi FG
                        %mo =
                        %mi GF
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} id
                          %mi K
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FG = GF = \operatorname{id}_{K}
            %li
              %p
                If this were adjoint, the triangle equality for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                will stipulate that
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi g
                          %mi ρ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %msubsup
                          %mi g
                          %mi σ
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(g_{\rho}) = g_{\sigma}^{-1}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mi ρ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_{\rho}
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mi σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_{\sigma}
                to break this. For example, let
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mi ρ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_{\rho}
                be
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1,1)
                and let
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mi σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_{\sigma}
                be
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,1)
          %p
            This is a special case of the fact that, given a non-adjoint equivalence, you can always replace its unit with another unit (which determines the counit) to improve the equivalence to an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+equivalence"} adjoint equivalence
          %h2#in_higher_categories In higher categories
          %p
            All of the above types of equivalence make sense for
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-category"} categories
            and
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+definition+of+higher+category"} algebraic definition of higher category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+definition+of+higher+category"} geometric definition of higher category
          %p
            In particular, one expects (although a proof depends on the exact definition and hasn't always been done) that in any
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            = succeed "-categories," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
            (in the sense of an
            = succeed "-category)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-surjective+functor"} surjective
            for all
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi k
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\le k\le n+1
            = succeed "-version" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %ul
            %li
              we have an axiom of choice and use weak (pseudo)
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-functor"} functors
            %li
              we use
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %span.newWikiWord
                anafunctors
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/n-anafunctor"} ?
              (which are automatically weak).
          %p
            If we use too strict a notion of
            = succeed "-functor," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-functors" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-functors" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            For example, assuming choice, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-functor"} strict 2-functor
            between strict
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Bicat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bicat
            if and only if it is essentially (up to equivalence) surjective on objects, locally essentially surjective, and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+fully+faithful+2-functor"} locally fully faithful
            %em strict
            = succeed "-functor," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            = succeed "-natural." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+3-category"} strict 3-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Str
                  %mn 2
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Str 2 Cat
            of
            = succeed "-categories," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            = succeed "-functors," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            = succeed "-natural" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %span.newWikiWord
              semi-strict 3-category
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/semi-strict+3-category"} ?
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Gray
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gray
            of strict
            = succeed "-categories," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            = succeed "-functors," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %p
            As with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Bicat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bicat
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full
            sub
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tricategory"} tricategory
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Gray
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gray
            by formally inverting all such weak equivalences. Note that even with the axiom of choice,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Bicat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bicat
            is
            %em not
            equivalent (as a tricategory) to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Gray
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gray
            %em some
            = succeed "-category;" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Gray
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gray
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0612299"} here
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %div
            %p
              %strong basic properties of…
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
                %ul
                  %li
                    %p
